   Manny looked at me.
   “JC.”
   “Don’t.”
   “You don’t get to decide that.”
   “I’m not deciding, I’m—”
   “You’re deciding.” His voice wasn’t loud. That’s how I knew.
“You’ve carried everything alone since we were nine. Your dad. The
rent. The thing with your head. Every time it got heavy, you said ‘long
night’ and went home. And I let you, because I’m not smart, I thought
that was how you were.” He set down the bag. “You know what that
is? That’s not brave. That’s lonely with a haircut.”
   I had nothing. I looked at my hands.
   “I’m not asking permission,” Manny said. “I’m telling you what I’m
doing. You can do it with me or you can do it around me, and I’ll show
up either way, and honestly it’s a lot of walking for me.”
   Somewhere on the tar, a pigeon made a remark.
   “Okay,” I said. “In.”
   “In.”
   “But you don’t go to anything of Vale’s without telling me first.”
   “Deal.” He picked up the bag again. “You tell me when you’re going
to carry something, too.”
   I said I would. I was lying. He knew. We both ate a tostón anyway.
   He’d brought the drum case up, as he always does, and set it flat on
the gravel between us. His palm went down on the lid. And the roof
changed.



It was night, and no light anywhere.
   July 1977. The whole city dark. Not a streetlight, not a window, not
the glow of a television; the borough lay black from here to the river,
and the sky was so thick with stars it looked like somebody’s mistake.
